WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:17.303
The Rangoon was as fast as the Mongolia, but not as comfortable for Aouda as Mr. Fogg would have liked.

2
00:00:17.304 --> 00:00:25.119
However, the trip from Calcutta to Hong Kong would take only ten or twelve days, and Aouda was not difficult to please.

3
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:31.570
During the first few days of the journey, Aouda became better acquainted with her protector, Mr. Fogg.

4
00:00:31.571 --> 00:00:37.458
She frequently expressed her deep gratitude for his efforts in saving her from a horrible fate.

5
00:00:37.459 --> 00:00:45.262
Mr. Fogg listened to her without the slightest apparent emotion, but he seemed to be always on the watch for her care and comfort.

6
00:00:45.263 --> 00:00:51.870
He visited her regularly each day, not so much to talk himself, but to sit and listen to her.

7
00:00:51.871 --> 00:00:55.697
He treated her with the strictest politeness.

8
00:00:55.698 --> 00:00:59.189
Aouda did not quite know what to make of him.

9
00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:04.211
Passepartout had given her some hints of his master's eccentricity.

10
00:01:04.212 --> 00:01:09.721
She had smiled when she heard of the wager that was sending the two men around the world.

11
00:01:09.722 --> 00:01:14.456
Detective Fix had managed to board the boat at the last possible moment.

12
00:01:14.457 --> 00:01:20.966
He had left orders that the warrant for Mr. Fogg's arrest should be forwarded to him in Hong Kong.

13
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:26.484
He was hoping to conceal his presence on the Rangoon until the end of the voyage.

14
00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:31.029
All Fix's hopes and wishes were now centered on Hong Kong.

15
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:37.850
The steamer would be stopping in Singapore, but the stay there would be too brief for him to arrest Mr. Fogg.

16
00:01:37.851 --> 00:01:47.662
"Now, either the warrant will be at Hong Kong, in which case I shall finally be able to arrest my man, or it will not be there," thought Fix.

17
00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:53.179
"If it's not there, it is absolutely necessary that I delay his departure.

18
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.466
I have failed at Bombay, and I have failed at Calcutta.

19
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:06.239
If I fail at Hong Kong, my reputation is lost! Cost what it may, I must succeed!"

20
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:13.073
As he puzzled over his predicament, Fix decided that he must make a confidant of Passepartout.

21
00:02:13.074 --> 00:02:18.022
He was certain that Passepartout was not Mr. Fogg's accomplice in crime.

22
00:02:18.023 --> 00:02:23.933
"I will tell him what kind of man his master is," Fix thought.

23
00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:34.189
"When Passepartout finds out how Mr. Fogg really makes his money, the servant will fear being accused himself and become my ally."

24
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:37.710
But this strategy was risky, Fix realized.

25
00:02:37.711 --> 00:02:43.260
"One word of warning from Passepartout to his master and all will be ruined!"

26
00:02:43.261 --> 00:02:48.492
There was no denying it—the detective was in a difficult position.

27
00:02:48.493 --> 00:02:54.216
One day Fix emerged from his cabin and went on deck to get some air.

28
00:02:54.217 --> 00:03:01.478
Passepartout was walking up and down in the forward part of the steamer when Fix rushed up to him, faking surprise.

29
00:03:01.479 --> 00:03:05.848
"You? Here, on the Rangoon?" he said.

30
00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:10.871
"What, Monsieur Fix, are you onboard?" said the astonished Passepartout.

31
00:03:10.872 --> 00:03:14.632
"Why, I left you at Bombay, and here you are, on the way to Hong Kong!

32
00:03:14.633 --> 00:03:16.886
Are you going around the world too?"

33
00:03:16.887 --> 00:03:23.650
"No, no," replied Fix. "I shall stop at Hong Kong, at least for some days."

34
00:03:23.651 --> 00:03:30.443
"Hmm," said Passepartout. "But why haven't I seen you onboard since we left Calcutta?"

35
00:03:30.444 --> 00:03:39.743
"Oh, a bit of sea sickness. I've been staying in my cabin," replied Fix. "And how is Mr. Fogg?"

36
00:03:39.744 --> 00:03:45.954
"As well and as punctual as ever," replied Passepartout. "Not a day behind time!"

37
00:03:45.955 --> 00:03:54.900
Passepartout went on to explain Aouda's presence among them, and Fix, as usual, invited Passepartout for a drink in the bar.

38
00:03:54.901 --> 00:04:03.654
Later that night as he lay in bed, Passepartout puzzled over the strange coincidence that had kept Fix on the same route as his master.

39
00:04:03.655 --> 00:04:10.084
Passepartout wondered why this very friendly person, whom he had met at Suez and then encountered onboard the Mongolia,

40
00:04:10.085 --> 00:04:17.203
now turned up unexpectedly on the Rangoon despite having told Passepartout that Bombay was his destination.

41
00:04:17.204 --> 00:04:21.038
Was he following Mr. Fogg step by step?

42
00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:29.650
Passepartout was ready to wager that Fix would also leave Hong Kong at the same time as Mr. Fogg and probably on the same steamer!

43
00:04:29.651 --> 00:04:34.483
Passepartout realized Fix must be an agent from the Reform Club.

44
00:04:34.484 --> 00:04:40.842
He must be a spy sent to make sure that Mr. Fogg really went around the world, as was agreed.

45
00:04:40.843 --> 00:04:45.774
"It's clear!" thought Passepartout, satisfied with his own shrewdness.

46
00:04:45.775 --> 00:04:51.954
"He has been sent to keep us in view! Mr. Fogg is such an honorable man!

47
00:04:51.955 --> 00:04:55.139
This is not the proper thing for them to do at all!

48
00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:01.753
Well, gentlemen of the Reform Club," decided Passepartout, "this shall cost you dearly."

49
00:05:01.754 --> 00:05:06.793
He was determined to tease Fix when he got the chance.

50
00:05:06.794 --> 00:05:13.999
On the afternoon of Wednesday, October 30, the Rangoon, now half a day ahead of schedule, arrived in Singapore.

51
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:21.954
Phileas Fogg noted this arrival in his journal and then walked ashore, accompanied by Aouda.

52
00:05:21.955 --> 00:05:28.455
I spent the last five days on a steamer called the Rangoon, traveling from Calcutta to Hong Kong.

53
00:05:28.456 --> 00:05:33.371
This steamer wasn't as luxurious as it could have been, but it wasn't too bad.

54
00:05:33.372 --> 00:05:39.006
Steamers, also known as steamboats or steamships, are the most common type of boat in the world.

55
00:05:39.007 --> 00:05:46.719
They are used not only to carry passengers, but also to transport goods such as coffee, tea, silk, and spices.

56
00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:53.494
In fact, steamers have allowed global trade to increase quite rapidly since they were first used in the early 1800s.

57
00:05:53.495 --> 00:05:59.599
Today steamers come in all shapes and sizes, and sail on canals, rivers, and oceans.

58
00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:06.460
They can travel large distances cheaply and safely.
